---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Button from "../components/Button.astro";
import Canvas from "../components/Canvas.astro";
import FlowSource from "../components/FlowSource.astro";
import Refusal from "../components/Refusal.astro";
import RunTable from "../components/RunTable.astro";
import Kinds from "../components/Kinds.astro";
import { siteScan, scanBody, unchecked, checked } from "../lib/flows";

const tasks = [
  {
    name: "Report QC",
    what: "Check a finished report against every source it cites and surface the inconsistencies.",
    failure: "An inconsistency the QC itself missed because it sampled.",
  },
  {
    name: "Source Validation",
    what: "Find candidate sources on a topic, grade each against five criteria, keep what passes.",
    failure: "An irrelevant source that slipped the criteria.",
  },
  {
    name: "Dataset Assembly",
    what: "Pull figures from filings and data vendors into one model, every cell traceable to its figure.",
    failure: "A number that no longer matches its source after an update.",
  },
  {
    name: "Evidence Combing",
    what: "Go through a thousand documents for evidence and counterevidence on a claim.",
    failure: "An unvetted quote, or a document silently skipped.",
  },
  {
    name: "Market Map",
    what: "Find every major player in an industry, how each is positioned, and what changed.",
    failure: "A missing player nobody notices is missing.",
  },
  {
    name: "Document Revision",
    what: "Take in a draft, gather what bears on it, and propose the changes as a redline.",
    failure: "A change nobody can trace to a reason.",
  },
];

const scanSource = `fn scan(row: Row) -> Scanned {
    let url    = Field::new(url_column).input(row);
    let page   = Source::new(fetch_page).input(url.text);
    let text   = Format::new(html_to_text).input(page.document);
    let issues = Summarize::new(issues_on_this_page).input(text.text);
    Scanned { issues: issues.text }
}`;
---

<BaseLayout>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <h1 class="display">Hand the work to agents. Keep the process in view.</h1>
      <p class="lead measure">
        Functionary is for research and analysis that an agent could do and
        that you are on the hook for. An agent writes the process as a flow of
        trusted steps. The flow is the program. You read it, run it, and fix
        the step that was wrong.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <Button href="/how-it-works" variant="primary">How it works</Button>
        <Button href="/self-hosting" variant="ghost">Deployment</Button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="container wide hero-canvas">
      <Canvas flow={siteScan} run bare />
      <p class="desktop-note">The canvas is a desktop tool. The argument reads fine here; the product does not.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>Control, not capability</h2>
        <p class="stack-p">
          The work is the kind an early-career consultant, research assistant
          or analyst does, and models have been able to do it for a couple of
          years. What they cannot give you is a process you can see: dozens to
          hundreds of steps, done the way you meant, the same way next week,
          with the record to show it. The person whose name goes on the result
          has no view of what happened below. That is why research shops pay
          for footnote checks and for replications of their own work.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="tasks">
        {tasks.map((t) => (
          <li>
            <span class="task-name">{t.name}</span>
            <p class="task-what">{t.what}</p>
            <p class="task-fail"><span>The failure</span>{t.failure}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="measure after">
        Maybe an agent plus an adversarial review loop never gets anything
        wrong. Maybe the mess is fine and the cost is trivial next to the
        benefit. Most people seem to think so, and they may be right.
        Functionary is for the people who are exposed if they are not.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container split">
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>Where the risk is</h2>
        <p>
          Research and analysis are sold on trust. The people who rely on the
          work take it on the strength of who produced it, and the bar is
          exact. The errors that break that trust are rarely bold ones. They
          are omissions: a step skipped, or a result carried forward as if it
          had been checked, with nothing in the finished output to show where.
          So in Functionary every value carries where it came from. A value a
          model wrote stays marked through every later step, and only a
          deterministic check against the source or a person clears it. The
          knowledge base refuses anything nobody cleared.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="pair">
        <Canvas flow={unchecked} legend caption="A model-written table heads for the knowledge base. The dashed wire says a model wrote it; the Write refuses it." />
        <Canvas flow={checked} legend caption="A Check confirms each quote is in the filing. The wire after it is verified, and the Write accepts it." />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>The flow is the program</h2>
        <p class="stack-p">
          You never read code. The agent never looks at a canvas. They are
          the same object in two forms: the canvas is drawn from the flow's
          source, and an edit on the canvas is written back into it. What you
          see is what the agent wrote, and what the agent wrote is what runs.
        </p>
        <p>
          The agent cannot write arbitrary code. It composes vetted blocks and
          the control structures the canvas can draw, in a fixed subset of
          Rust, and anything else is refused before it is saved. There is no
          hard-coded data, no file read that is not a step, and no second way
          to execute.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="bijection">
        <div>
          <p class="side">You see</p>
          <Canvas flow={scanBody} />
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="side">The agent sees</p>
          <FlowSource code={scanSource} file="weekly-scan.rs" />
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="refusal-wrap">
        <Refusal />
        <p class="fig-note">What the agent may not write: an improvised loop and a bare file read. Both are refused, and the diagnostic says which step to use instead.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>One judgement per step</h2>
        <p class="stack-p">
          A block does one judgement: summarize this page and keep track of the
          source text, pull the passages that bear on a question, grade this
          source against these criteria. Its prompt is tuned by us and never
          edited by the agent that places it. The model inside can only take
          the actions the block lists. That is the product: being able to say
          with confidence that the only thing this step can do is read its
          input and look at the documents in front of it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every model call, tool call, review decision and write leaves a
          receipt. A failed row is a failed row in the result, not a blank.
          Test runs the whole flow and withholds the writes, so you read the
          outcome before anything leaves.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <RunTable
          title="Weekly scan"
          subtitle="Run table · Test"
          badge="result"
          tint="var(--cat-synthesis)"
          columns={["site", "fetched", "summary", "receipt"]}
          rows={[
            [{ text: "example-vendor.com/pricing" }, { text: "200 · 41 KB", origin: "sourced" }, { text: "Two plans removed since last scan; enterprise tier now quote-only.", origin: "generated" }, { text: "sha256:9f2a1c…" }],
            [{ text: "competitor.io/changelog" }, { text: "200 · 128 KB", origin: "sourced" }, { text: "Three releases; one deprecates the export API.", origin: "generated" }, { text: "sha256:41bc07…" }],
            [{ text: "partner.example/terms" }, { text: "403", status: "failed" }, { text: "", status: "missing" }, { text: "sha256:7d01e8…" }],
          ]}
          caption="One row per site. The third site refused the fetch; the row says so instead of disappearing."
        />
      </div>
      <div class="kinds-wrap">
        <Kinds />
        <p class="fig-note">The palette. Seven kinds of step, and the verbs under each.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>Iterate, then stabilize</h2>
        <p class="stack-p">
          Most flows get built, adjusted, run, adjusted, run, and then rarely
          touched again. That loop carries no versioning ceremony. One person
          creates, edits, runs and reviews, and never sees the word pin.
        </p>
        <p>
          When a flow starts to matter, you ask for friction. Every run records
          exactly which revision it ran. A correction is a change to a visible
          step, with its history, and the next run inherits it. The process
          improves. The model does not have to.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="ladder">
        <div>
          <h3>Iterate</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Describe the job; the agent drafts the flow.</li>
            <li>Test. Read every row. Change a setting, delete a step.</li>
            <li>Run it again with new inputs next week.</li>
            <li>Imports follow the latest version on their own.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Stabilize</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Pin the tables and modules the flow reads.</li>
            <li>Name who reviews its outputs, and where.</li>
            <li>Require an approved revision before an unattended run.</li>
            <li>Start it on a schedule, a webhook, or an inbound email.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section cta">
    <div class="container measure">
      <h2>Own the process</h2>
      <p class="stack-p">
        Flows are source files in a project you own. Runs, receipts and the
        knowledge base live under one data directory. Models and connected
        systems are steps on the canvas, so you can read what leaves.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <Button href="/how-it-works" variant="primary">How it works</Button>
        <Button href="/self-hosting" variant="ghost">Deployment</Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding-block: clamp(3.5rem, 8vh, 6rem) var(--section-pad);
  }
  h1.display {
    max-width: 24ch;
    hyphens: manual;
    overflow-wrap: normal;
  }
  .lead {
    margin-top: var(--s-24);
  }
  .actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--s-12);
    margin-top: var(--s-32);
  }
  .hero-canvas {
    margin-top: var(--s-40);
  }
  .desktop-note {
    display: none;
    margin-top: var(--s-12);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-11);
    color: var(--fg-3);
  }

  h2 {
    max-width: 24ch;
  }
  .stack-p {
    margin-bottom: var(--s-16);
  }
  .section p {
    margin-top: var(--s-16);
  }
  .after {
    margin-top: var(--s-32);
  }

  .tasks {
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--s-32) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--s-16);
  }
  .tasks li {
    border: 1px solid var(--border-0);
    border-radius: var(--r-8);
    background: var(--bg-1);
    padding: var(--s-16) var(--s-20) var(--s-20);
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-8);
    align-content: start;
  }
  .task-name {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-12);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--fg-0);
  }
  .tasks p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--ms-small);
    color: var(--fg-1);
  }
  .task-fail {
    color: var(--fg-2);
    padding-top: var(--s-8);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-0);
  }
  .task-fail span {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-10);
    color: var(--danger);
    margin-bottom: 2px;
  }

  .split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr);
    gap: var(--s-40);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .pair {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-24);
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .bijection {
    margin-top: var(--s-40);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-24);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .side {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-11);
    color: var(--fg-2);
    margin: 0 0 var(--s-10);
  }
  .bijection > div {
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .refusal-wrap {
    margin-top: var(--s-32);
    max-width: 56rem;
  }
  .fig-note {
    font-size: var(--ms-small);
    color: var(--fg-2);
  }
  .table-wrap,
  .kinds-wrap {
    margin-top: var(--s-40);
  }

  .ladder {
    margin-top: var(--s-32);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--s-20);
    max-width: 56rem;
  }
  .ladder div {
    border-top: 2px solid var(--border-1);
    padding-top: var(--s-12);
  }
  .ladder div:last-child {
    border-top-color: var(--cat-human);
  }
  .ladder h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-12);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--fg-0);
  }
  .ladder ul {
    margin: var(--s-10) 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.1em;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-6);
    font-size: var(--ms-small);
    color: var(--fg-1);
  }
  .ladder li::marker {
    color: var(--fg-3);
  }

  .cta {
    background: var(--bg-1);
  }

  @media (max-width: 1000px) {
    .tasks {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .split,
    .bijection,
    .ladder {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
    .desktop-note {
      display: block;
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .tasks {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    h1.display {
      font-size: clamp(2rem, 1rem + 6vw, 2.55rem);
    }
  }
</style>
